1
00:01:47,149 --> 00:01:50,736
The general talked about
killing the president.

2
00:01:50,803 --> 00:01:53,139
That, along with Conway's
outburst on the plane...

3
00:01:53,238 --> 00:01:54,811
That sounds worse than it was.

4
00:01:54,836 --> 00:01:56,849
No, that sounds exactly
as bad as it was.

5
00:01:56,909 --> 00:01:58,576
What's surprising to me is...

6
00:01:58,661 --> 00:02:00,412
the campaign couldn't get
their hands around this.

7
00:02:00,495 --> 00:02:02,539
I mean, Harry Marshall is
as good as they come, but...

8
00:02:02,623 --> 00:02:05,333
First they denied, and
then they equivocated...

9
00:02:05,417 --> 00:02:06,919
I mean, that's just, like, ridiculous.

10
00:02:07,002 --> 00:02:09,421
You just come out, "I apologize.
We misspoke."

11
00:02:09,504 --> 00:02:10,588
Everybody knows the drill here.

12
00:02:10,673 --> 00:02:13,300
And it's not like the Republicans
rushed in to save the day either.

13
00:02:13,383 --> 00:02:16,178
- Uh, off message throughout.
- They got quiet.

14
00:02:16,261 --> 00:02:18,596
Which I will never understand.

15
00:02:18,681 --> 00:02:22,225
Will Conway was a rising
star in the party.

16
00:02:22,309 --> 00:02:25,187
Look, it's hard to compete
with the Underwood machine.

17
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:28,273
Oh, God. Let's not do the whole
"black helicopters" crazy stuff.

18
00:02:28,356 --> 00:02:29,399
It's ridiculous.

19
00:02:29,482 --> 00:02:32,110
Look, we need to accept
Underwood won the election.

20
00:02:32,194 --> 00:02:33,987
People need to accept the
outcome and move on.

21
00:02:34,071 --> 00:02:35,572
I'm gonna agree with Van.

22
00:02:35,656 --> 00:02:39,076
I think we need to take a moment
to appreciate the Constitution.

23
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:42,079
- It bent, but it never broke.
- That's right.

24
00:02:42,162 --> 00:02:45,666
And in the end, as we all know, it
came down to the Electoral College.

25
00:02:46,499 --> 00:02:49,419
<i>You don't
have to say the words, Governor.</i>

26
00:02:52,339 --> 00:02:53,924
I think I do.

27
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:59,930
Congratulations, Mr. President Elect.

28
00:03:01,514 --> 00:03:05,268
- There's no script for this one?
<i>- Country first, sir.</i>

29
00:03:05,352 --> 00:03:07,229
I'm glad to hear you say that, Will.

30
00:03:07,312 --> 00:03:11,274
I want to start to help
the country heal again.

31
00:03:11,358 --> 00:03:12,735
<i>Are we done?</i>

32
00:03:12,818 --> 00:03:16,071
We are done. But Will...

33
00:03:18,031 --> 00:03:20,937
I would very much like
to show the country

34
00:03:20,962 --> 00:03:23,394
that our two parties can work together.

35
00:03:23,453 --> 00:03:29,877
So I was hoping that you might consider
becoming my transportation secretary.

36
00:03:29,960 --> 00:03:34,589
<i>I think it would send a powerful message
if you agreed to join my cabinet.</i>

37
00:03:35,423 --> 00:03:41,096
You think I'd... make a good...
transportation secretary?

38
00:03:41,179 --> 00:03:43,140
Oh, yes.

39
00:03:43,223 --> 00:03:45,809
<i>That's what you give the
congressman from nowhere.</i>

40
00:03:45,893 --> 00:03:46,810
I'll be declining.

41
00:03:46,894 --> 00:03:50,688
<i>Well, I'm so sorry to hear that, Will.
And I wanted to wish you the very best.</i>

42
00:03:50,773 --> 00:03:53,566
You and your wishes can
go fuck yourselves!

43
00:04:06,371 --> 00:04:07,831
What do I do now?

44
00:04:07,915 --> 00:04:09,124
<i>...everyone
thought it would happen.</i>

45
00:04:09,207 --> 00:04:11,084
<i>No one thought that Underwood
would win again...</i>

46
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:13,336
I don't know.

47
00:04:17,590 --> 00:04:21,053
Acknowledging
defeat is never easy.

48
00:04:21,136 --> 00:04:24,264
Doing it in public is a living hell.

49
00:04:25,265 --> 00:04:28,560
Well, at least the cameras like him.

50
00:04:36,234 --> 00:04:38,320
Do I look satisfied?

51
00:04:39,112 --> 00:04:41,573
You look somewhat satisfied.

52
00:04:41,656 --> 00:04:45,786
- Good.
- Not good. Francis, enjoy this moment.

53
00:04:45,869 --> 00:04:49,372
- You deserve it.
- You mean "we."

54
00:04:50,457 --> 00:04:52,793
We made it to the other side.

55
00:04:53,418 --> 00:04:54,586
Yes.

56
00:04:56,004 --> 00:04:57,840
But what is on the other side?

57
00:04:59,049 --> 00:05:00,175
Victory.

58
00:05:01,259 --> 00:05:02,928
With a whiff of defeat.

59
00:05:04,512 --> 00:05:08,641
It's taken a long time to get here,
but we're here. Cheers, Francis.

60
00:05:09,767 --> 00:05:12,520
Everything gets easier from now on.

61
00:05:13,396 --> 00:05:14,522
Mmm.

62
00:05:27,870 --> 00:05:29,121
They're ready for you.

63
00:05:33,876 --> 00:05:36,044
- Hannah.
- I don't want to go.

64
00:05:50,517 --> 00:05:53,228
- Hey.
- No.

65
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:01,611
Fine.

66
00:06:02,946 --> 00:06:04,489
I'll do it on my own.

67
00:06:04,572 --> 00:06:08,618
I'm gonna say, this is a crucial moment.
For both of you. How you handle tonight.

68
00:06:08,701 --> 00:06:12,039
The party abandoned me. Your party.

69
00:06:13,123 --> 00:06:15,208
I'm not the Republican Party, Will.

70
00:06:16,543 --> 00:06:18,336
I work for candidates.

71
00:06:19,337 --> 00:06:20,672
I worked for you.

72
00:06:21,965 --> 00:06:23,758
Listen, you lost.

73
00:06:23,841 --> 00:06:27,429
But more than half the country still
considers you their president.

74
00:06:27,512 --> 00:06:29,764
This is what's required. Go
down there, give your speech.

75
00:06:33,393 --> 00:06:34,644
Concede.

76
00:07:52,514 --> 00:07:55,767
As I accept all of your
letters of resignation

77
00:07:55,850 --> 00:07:57,810
and dissolve my old cabinet,

78
00:07:58,395 --> 00:08:02,190
I wanna welcome each and every
one of you to my new cabinet.

79
00:08:06,028 --> 00:08:09,322
- Congratulations, Mr. President.
- Thank you, Cathy.

80
00:08:09,406 --> 00:08:11,533
And I especially wanna thank my wife.

81
00:08:13,701 --> 00:08:15,037
Madam Vice President...

82
00:08:15,662 --> 00:08:19,541
thank you for your unending
and unwavering support.

83
00:08:19,624 --> 00:08:24,004
Your steady hand kept this
country running this past month.

84
00:08:25,005 --> 00:08:29,426
I owe her more than I can
possibly fathom. And she owes me.

85
00:08:30,427 --> 00:08:34,389
Not true. You were with me all the way.

86
00:08:35,432 --> 00:08:40,603
I would like to make my first 100
days as the duly elected president

87
00:08:40,687 --> 00:08:44,482
as productive as possible. Of
course, there are many challenges.

88
00:08:44,566 --> 00:08:48,111
Shoring up the economy,
securing our nation from ICO

89
00:08:48,195 --> 00:08:51,864
and reducing unemployment. But the
biggest challenge that we face,

90
00:08:51,948 --> 00:08:55,368
and the one that I'm going
to need all of your help on,

91
00:08:55,452 --> 00:09:00,498
is reuniting a country that has never
been more divided than it is today.

92
00:09:01,374 --> 00:09:03,918
I believe we can meet this challenge,

93
00:09:04,001 --> 00:09:06,296
and that's where I'm gonna
need all of your help.

94
00:09:06,379 --> 00:09:11,926
So let's let the healing begin today
and let's make history together.

95
00:09:12,009 --> 00:09:14,429
I thank you all for
your continued service.

96
00:09:14,512 --> 00:09:16,139
Now let's get back to work.

97
00:09:33,281 --> 00:09:34,824
I need a minute.

98
00:09:35,825 --> 00:09:40,037
I think we should see what we can do
about making Usher persona non grata.

99
00:09:40,122 --> 00:09:43,291
I don't think that's wise to start a
war. We've just won the White House.

100
00:09:43,375 --> 00:09:45,627
This is exactly when we should strike.

101
00:09:45,710 --> 00:09:47,379
This is a moment that we
have the political capital...

102
00:09:47,462 --> 00:09:50,465
Usher needs to be managed carefully.

103
00:09:50,548 --> 00:09:52,049
Mr. President?

104
00:09:52,134 --> 00:09:55,428
Take a breath, Doug. We've only
just landed on our feet here.

105
00:09:55,512 --> 00:09:57,222
Sir, Madam Vice President,

106
00:09:57,305 --> 00:09:59,974
this is the type of person that
we used to crucify in the House.

107
00:10:00,057 --> 00:10:02,227
Yes, but we're not in the House now.
We're here.

108
00:10:02,810 --> 00:10:06,022
We have a full term ahead of us.
There's gonna be a great deal

109
00:10:06,106 --> 00:10:08,150
that we're gonna be able to
accomplish, the three of us.

110
00:10:09,359 --> 00:10:11,403
I appreciate that, sir.

111
00:10:11,486 --> 00:10:13,905
But Mark Usher will become a problem.

112
00:10:13,988 --> 00:10:17,492
Well, everyone becomes
a problem, eventually.

113
00:10:20,203 --> 00:10:22,205
LeAnn's out.

114
00:10:22,955 --> 00:10:24,081
What?

115
00:10:26,834 --> 00:10:27,919
Why?

116
00:10:33,341 --> 00:10:34,467
She screwed up.

117
00:10:35,426 --> 00:10:37,262
Well, is it something I
need to get ready for?

118
00:10:37,345 --> 00:10:39,806
The Underwoods want this
handled indirectly.

119
00:10:40,515 --> 00:10:42,225
So what exactly are we saying?

120
00:10:42,809 --> 00:10:44,311
She's being pushed out.

121
00:10:44,394 --> 00:10:48,273
Okay, but the campaign's over.
Is it necessary to say anything?

122
00:10:48,356 --> 00:10:53,320
We need to distance this administration
from her and her work. Her tactics.

123
00:10:54,779 --> 00:10:56,406
So it needs to be a leak.

124
00:10:56,489 --> 00:10:59,992
Reach out to the people who don't like
us. They'll be prime to go with it.

125
00:11:00,076 --> 00:11:02,162
Okay, so when we respond...

126
00:11:02,245 --> 00:11:04,164
- On the record or...
- On.

127
00:11:06,958 --> 00:11:08,210
What did she do?

128
00:11:08,876 --> 00:11:11,379
She showed some very poor judgment.

129
00:11:12,004 --> 00:11:14,090
I'm trusting you to be discreet.

130
00:11:15,633 --> 00:11:18,678
We won, Seth. Changes need to be made.

131
00:11:21,389 --> 00:11:24,309
You know I'm off politics.
Surprised you reached out to me.

132
00:11:24,392 --> 00:11:26,936
You're a
highly-respected journalist.

133
00:11:27,019 --> 00:11:28,938
Just here for the
inauguration and the parties.

134
00:11:29,021 --> 00:11:30,022
Hmm.

135
00:11:31,148 --> 00:11:34,402
So I imagine you've heard
that I'm on the outs.

136
00:11:34,486 --> 00:11:36,446
They're not telling the whole story.

137
00:11:37,989 --> 00:11:39,031
So what happened?

138
00:11:39,115 --> 00:11:43,119
An acquaintance of mine stole
classified documents from the NSA

139
00:11:43,202 --> 00:11:44,704
and took off to Russia.

140
00:11:45,997 --> 00:11:47,749
They're blaming me for it.

141
00:11:49,876 --> 00:11:51,753
What did this acquaintance take?

142
00:11:52,670 --> 00:11:55,798
That's just the thing. I have no idea.

143
00:11:56,549 --> 00:11:58,843
If you could find him, talk to him,

144
00:11:58,926 --> 00:12:02,096
he would tell you that I had
nothing to do with this.

145
00:12:08,270 --> 00:12:10,062
Who is he? What's his name?

146
00:12:12,148 --> 00:12:13,275
Aidan Macallan.

147
00:12:13,358 --> 00:12:16,278
He'll talk to you. He respects you.

148
00:12:18,696 --> 00:12:20,615
Tell him that I'm okay.

149
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:23,618
That I'm going to be okay.

150
00:12:25,077 --> 00:12:26,871
I'm a firm believer in
boots on the ground.

151
00:12:26,954 --> 00:12:29,165
Well, we're there for the oil.
Let's not pretend.

152
00:12:29,248 --> 00:12:32,752
Oh, I'm not denying how crucial
it is to protect the oil supply.

153
00:12:32,835 --> 00:12:34,545
But it's in the world's best interest

154
00:12:34,608 --> 00:12:36,279
that we're the ones
keeping an eye on it.

155
00:12:36,339 --> 00:12:39,133
- On the world or the oil?
- Both.

156
00:12:39,216 --> 00:12:43,054
Oh. Well, that explains your
strong interest in Antarctica.

157
00:12:43,137 --> 00:12:46,641
I do focus on things and people.

158
00:12:46,724 --> 00:12:50,395
I tend to get attached, but I think
it makes me better at what I do.

159
00:12:51,396 --> 00:12:53,022
And what are you focused on now?

160
00:12:53,105 --> 00:12:57,610
Petrov. Ahmed Al Ahmadi. To begin with.

161
00:13:02,865 --> 00:13:04,033
Oh, I'm... I'm sorry.

162
00:13:06,243 --> 00:13:10,498
I just... Uh... I'll run
this by you tomorrow.

163
00:13:11,833 --> 00:13:13,376
Yes, tomorrow.

164
00:13:15,545 --> 00:13:17,213
Sorry for the interruption.

165
00:13:19,757 --> 00:13:21,551
He's one of our speechwriters.

166
00:13:22,552 --> 00:13:24,762
Tom Yates has quite a reputation.

167
00:13:26,013 --> 00:13:29,642
Well, if his books are any indication,
he has lived quite a life.

168
00:13:30,226 --> 00:13:32,311
My father wore striped socks.

169
00:13:34,856 --> 00:13:38,234
Do you think the president will
be sympathetic to our agenda?

170
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:41,988
"Our agenda"? That's very presumptuous.

171
00:13:42,071 --> 00:13:44,392
Well, there are regions
in the world that are

172
00:13:44,417 --> 00:13:46,182
important to our national security

173
00:13:46,242 --> 00:13:47,952
and they have to be protected.

174
00:13:48,035 --> 00:13:50,287
Would you like to join us
at the inaugural ball?

175
00:13:50,372 --> 00:13:51,498
Oh, no, I can't.

176
00:13:52,248 --> 00:13:54,917
Something terrible always
happens when I go to a party.

177
00:13:57,795 --> 00:14:01,633
Uh, by the way, my husband
is not managed by anyone.

178
00:14:01,716 --> 00:14:04,427
- That's not what I'm asking.
- Isn't it?

179
00:14:04,511 --> 00:14:08,931
I'm asking for your help, Madam
Vice President. That is all.

180
00:14:14,103 --> 00:14:15,104
Evening.

181
00:14:15,187 --> 00:14:19,275
Oh, my God. Come in. I'm sorry.
Sit down, sit down.

182
00:14:19,359 --> 00:14:20,568
Aren't we working out tonight?

183
00:14:20,652 --> 00:14:23,571
Oh, I just don't think I'm
gonna have the time for it.

184
00:14:23,654 --> 00:14:26,783
- But come sit for a minute.
- If you're too busy, I understand...

185
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:29,202
No, no. Join me for a minute.
I want you to hear something.

186
00:14:38,169 --> 00:14:41,548
"This is the dawn of a new era.

187
00:14:42,339 --> 00:14:43,841
It's a time for peace.

188
00:14:43,925 --> 00:14:48,471
A time to respect the past
and embrace the future.

189
00:14:48,555 --> 00:14:49,806
A time..."

190
00:14:58,440 --> 00:14:59,482
Time to what?

191
00:14:59,566 --> 00:15:03,069
"A time to hear all the voices.

192
00:15:03,152 --> 00:15:05,988
For there is nothing more lonely...

193
00:15:07,949 --> 00:15:09,659
or terrifying...

194
00:15:12,662 --> 00:15:14,246
than feeling unheard."

195
00:15:18,167 --> 00:15:21,754
- What do you think?
- It's interesting. I like it.

196
00:15:22,422 --> 00:15:25,299
- This is for...
- My inauguration, yes.

197
00:15:26,634 --> 00:15:28,135
Where does it go from there?
What do you say?

198
00:15:28,219 --> 00:15:30,888
Uh, uh...

199
00:15:32,557 --> 00:15:34,684
"America, I hear you.

200
00:15:35,643 --> 00:15:39,939
Whether you voted for me or
not, I hear all of you."

201
00:15:42,024 --> 00:15:44,652
Now, what do you think my
great-great-great-grandfather

202
00:15:44,736 --> 00:15:46,403
would think of all this?

203
00:15:49,491 --> 00:15:52,076
Awesome.

204
00:15:52,994 --> 00:15:55,412
- In the original sense of the word.
- Yeah.

205
00:15:57,624 --> 00:15:59,125
How long you in town for?

206
00:16:00,001 --> 00:16:01,753
Leaving tonight, actually.

207
00:16:01,836 --> 00:16:03,630
I thought you were here
for the inauguration.

208
00:16:03,713 --> 00:16:07,299
- I got a tip I have to move on.
- Mmm. What's the tip?

209
00:16:07,383 --> 00:16:09,093
Mmm-mmm.

210
00:16:09,176 --> 00:16:11,471
We used to have a rule about shoptalk.

211
00:16:11,554 --> 00:16:12,764
- Did we?
- Mmm-hmm.

212
00:16:12,847 --> 00:16:14,348
Fair enough.

213
00:16:18,853 --> 00:16:20,522
What's your read on LeAnn Harvey?

214
00:16:22,064 --> 00:16:24,984
That she's hard to read.
That's what I like about her.

215
00:16:25,067 --> 00:16:27,111
You trust her?

216
00:16:28,696 --> 00:16:29,864
Kate, come on.

217
00:16:36,412 --> 00:16:38,039
You look tired.

218
00:16:38,915 --> 00:16:40,583
I'm working eight days a week.

219
00:16:41,584 --> 00:16:45,838
I mean, you always looked tired, but now
you look tired in a... not sexy way.

220
00:16:47,757 --> 00:16:49,216
Sorry to disappoint.

221
00:16:49,300 --> 00:16:51,260
I actually have to get
back there in a minute.

222
00:16:51,343 --> 00:16:54,138
Oh, it's little late to head
back to the office, right?

223
00:16:57,850 --> 00:16:59,894
I've been there since last Tuesday.

224
00:17:01,478 --> 00:17:02,647
Honestly...

225
00:17:03,606 --> 00:17:05,232
I know this sounds weird...

226
00:17:06,901 --> 00:17:08,444
There's no place I'd rather be.

227
00:17:12,489 --> 00:17:16,452
The way you say that sounds so... rote.

228
00:17:17,119 --> 00:17:18,621
I don't know...

229
00:17:21,207 --> 00:17:23,167
What am I supposed to say to that?

230
00:17:26,170 --> 00:17:28,089
I just want to know how
you're doing, that's all.

231
00:17:29,215 --> 00:17:32,218
I'm just asking about you,
this person I'm sitting with.

232
00:17:40,643 --> 00:17:42,061
Where'd you go, Tom Yates?

233
00:17:53,405 --> 00:17:55,617
- Hi.
- I was looking for you.

234
00:17:56,492 --> 00:17:58,077
Not very hard, then.

235
00:17:58,911 --> 00:18:00,246
I need to talk to you.

236
00:18:02,289 --> 00:18:03,415
Okay.

237
00:18:04,291 --> 00:18:05,376
I'm done.

238
00:18:07,754 --> 00:18:10,923
With the speechwriting, I
don't want to do it anymore.

239
00:18:12,008 --> 00:18:14,343
I want to get back to some
other stuff I was working on.

240
00:18:14,426 --> 00:18:18,555
No offense. I mean, I know
writing speeches is writing...

241
00:18:19,473 --> 00:18:21,267
but it's not what I really wanna do.

242
00:18:25,855 --> 00:18:26,981
Okay.

243
00:18:29,441 --> 00:18:30,442
You sure?

244
00:18:32,153 --> 00:18:33,154
Yes.

245
00:18:35,865 --> 00:18:37,533
We'll transition you out.

246
00:18:39,035 --> 00:18:41,495
But you'll need to have
a reason to be here.

247
00:18:49,154 --> 00:18:51,865
Special advisor? Are you kidding me?

248
00:18:51,964 --> 00:18:53,229
There's speculation
that he's already...

249
00:18:53,254 --> 00:18:54,525
It's not speculation, Seth.

250
00:18:54,550 --> 00:18:57,178
People know that Mark Usher is
working for us in some capacity.

251
00:18:57,261 --> 00:18:59,346
- I mean, isn't that enough?
- Clean up this release.

252
00:18:59,430 --> 00:19:01,849
Uh, reaching across party
lines, that sort of thing.

253
00:19:01,933 --> 00:19:03,475
- And get it ready, just in case.
- Of course.

254
00:19:03,559 --> 00:19:04,560
That's it.

255
00:19:08,147 --> 00:19:10,399
I know you're not happy about this.

256
00:19:10,691 --> 00:19:12,060
What has he done to earn the title?

257
00:19:12,085 --> 00:19:13,426
This is not about him earning anything.

258
00:19:13,485 --> 00:19:17,198
He's already getting classified memos.
Why does she want this?

259
00:19:18,908 --> 00:19:19,909
"She"?

260
00:19:23,037 --> 00:19:25,164
I'm sorry. The vice president.

261
00:19:25,247 --> 00:19:28,918
What she wants is for this
administration to succeed.

262
00:19:29,001 --> 00:19:32,714
And what I want is for you to
befriend him. Get to know him.

263
00:19:32,797 --> 00:19:36,467
We want him to think he's managing
us while we're busy managing him.

264
00:19:39,386 --> 00:19:42,056
She has been pursuing Francis
Underwood for a long time.

265
00:19:42,139 --> 00:19:44,308
- I don't wanna talk to her.
- Aidan...

266
00:19:44,392 --> 00:19:45,851
How does she even know I'm here?

267
00:19:45,935 --> 00:19:48,145
Miss Baldwin is a very good journalist.

268
00:19:48,229 --> 00:19:51,774
Yeah, going public
will not help you, or me.

269
00:19:51,858 --> 00:19:55,361
You'll tell her how you manipulated
the American mood, hmm?

270
00:19:55,444 --> 00:19:58,530
- How you helped steal an election.
- That would be it for me.

271
00:19:58,614 --> 00:20:02,076
You're a valued guest
of the Russian state.

272
00:20:02,869 --> 00:20:04,245
I want my passport back.

273
00:20:04,328 --> 00:20:07,164
An American passport is the last
thing that will keep you safe.

274
00:20:07,248 --> 00:20:08,791
I'll take my chances.

275
00:20:13,254 --> 00:20:16,257
This will make you famous, hmm?
A whistle-blower.

276
00:20:16,340 --> 00:20:17,549
I don't wanna be famous.

277
00:20:19,593 --> 00:20:21,637
But you do want to be free.

278
00:20:29,645 --> 00:20:33,399
You spent $15,000 at The Capital Grille?
What the fuck are you eating?

279
00:20:33,482 --> 00:20:36,068
- This is Romero using the House Ethics
- It's a liability.

280
00:20:36,152 --> 00:20:37,778
And you should know better.

281
00:20:38,612 --> 00:20:40,114
How did you not disclose this, Terry?

282
00:20:40,197 --> 00:20:42,366
I want to pull him off his
committee assignments.

283
00:20:42,449 --> 00:20:44,952
Put him on Science. See how
he likes NASA briefings.

284
00:20:45,036 --> 00:20:46,746
I'd like to hold off on that.

285
00:20:47,288 --> 00:20:49,623
Romero's about to become
an ally to the president.

286
00:20:51,375 --> 00:20:52,376
And why's that?

287
00:20:52,960 --> 00:20:55,629
Because he was able to
find out about your tab.

288
00:20:55,712 --> 00:20:57,924
It's because he's relentless.

289
00:20:58,007 --> 00:21:02,178
Because in four years, 35% of the
vote in Arizona will be Latino.

290
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:05,472
Because he's the future.

291
00:21:09,268 --> 00:21:10,895
This is how it starts.

292
00:21:12,897 --> 00:21:14,606
I gotta get back to the Hill.

293
00:21:15,900 --> 00:21:17,026
You know, Doug...

294
00:21:18,235 --> 00:21:21,488
you and I, we've been around long
enough to see the guard change.

295
00:21:21,572 --> 00:21:25,159
And it changes because the people at
the top forget how they got there.

296
00:21:26,493 --> 00:21:28,996
Romero's trying to
change the guard, Doug.

297
00:21:29,080 --> 00:21:30,873
And we are the guard.

298
00:21:36,796 --> 00:21:40,132
If Congressman Womack has nothing
to hide, he has nothing to fear.

299
00:21:41,092 --> 00:21:43,803
We all have something
to hide, Congressman Romero.

300
00:21:44,386 --> 00:21:46,513
It's time for a change
in the leadership.

301
00:21:48,140 --> 00:21:50,893
The president would like
to be a friend to you,

302
00:21:50,977 --> 00:21:52,979
but you're making it very difficult.

303
00:21:56,773 --> 00:21:59,986
We can guarantee the immigration ban
will be lifted in the next week.

304
00:22:00,069 --> 00:22:02,113
Come on. Are we doing this again?

305
00:22:02,196 --> 00:22:04,115
You offer me some racist gesture.

306
00:22:04,198 --> 00:22:08,160
And I, what? I'm a friend forever?
Ban isn't even sustainable.

307
00:22:08,244 --> 00:22:09,495
What matters to you?

308
00:22:09,578 --> 00:22:11,163
- The president's attention.
- You have it.

309
00:22:11,247 --> 00:22:13,666
- His full attention...
- You know, that's enough.

310
00:22:18,963 --> 00:22:20,882
What is it that you really want?

311
00:22:21,465 --> 00:22:24,093
I want Medicare coverage
bumped up to where it was.

312
00:22:25,386 --> 00:22:28,264
There are retirees in my district.

313
00:22:28,347 --> 00:22:31,308
We're here now because you
want a bump in Medicare?

314
00:22:31,392 --> 00:22:33,019
Come on, Congressman.

315
00:22:34,186 --> 00:22:35,980
And two seats to the inauguration.

316
00:22:36,063 --> 00:22:38,399
- We are two days out.
- On the dais.

317
00:22:38,482 --> 00:22:40,943
You are going about this entirely wrong.

318
00:22:41,527 --> 00:22:45,406
You came here. You asked me.

319
00:22:45,489 --> 00:22:46,908
I'm telling you.

320
00:22:46,991 --> 00:22:49,326
I want to be there when
Francis Underwood says to me

321
00:22:49,410 --> 00:22:51,370
that there will be an
increase in Medicare.

322
00:22:51,453 --> 00:22:53,122
He does that, I'm his friend.

323
00:22:53,205 --> 00:22:56,708
Well, I think I speak for the president
when I say that's not going to happen.

324
00:22:56,792 --> 00:22:59,962
Mr. Stamper, I really appreciate
all the work you do for him,

325
00:23:00,046 --> 00:23:04,008
but you and I know that
my bloc is growing.

326
00:23:04,091 --> 00:23:06,969
All I'm looking for is
a line in his address.

327
00:23:09,471 --> 00:23:10,973
Two seats and a line.

328
00:23:12,385 --> 00:23:13,705
The balls on him.

329
00:23:13,839 --> 00:23:15,585
I told him it
was highly doubtful.

330
00:23:15,644 --> 00:23:18,897
- I can get back to him with a firm no.
- Or you could give him a line.

331
00:23:18,981 --> 00:23:21,192
His promises are unreliable.

332
00:23:21,275 --> 00:23:24,570
Look, I know Alex. We met
on Conway's campaign.

333
00:23:24,653 --> 00:23:25,904
Once he's in, he's in.

334
00:23:25,988 --> 00:23:29,116
Sir, we have to be very careful
with what we're giving away.

335
00:23:29,176 --> 00:23:31,004
He's capable of giving
this administration

336
00:23:31,029 --> 00:23:32,260
an enormous amount of grief.

337
00:23:32,285 --> 00:23:34,395
He's asking for a gesture.

338
00:23:34,455 --> 00:23:36,582
Oh, I'd say it's a
little more than that.

339
00:23:36,665 --> 00:23:39,418
I completely agree. He's going
after the people who got us here.

340
00:23:39,501 --> 00:23:40,962
He's not wrong, is he?

341
00:23:41,045 --> 00:23:43,005
There are some in leadership
whose time has come and gone.

342
00:23:43,880 --> 00:23:48,635
Look... I'm just here to make sure your
four years turn into eight, but...

343
00:23:48,719 --> 00:23:50,387
it's up to you, of course.

344
00:23:51,638 --> 00:23:55,726
But we're talking about two seats...
one line...

345
00:23:58,604 --> 00:23:59,981
Give him what he wants.

346
00:24:07,613 --> 00:24:08,739
Alex.

347
00:24:09,656 --> 00:24:11,867
Tilda. It's lovely to see you.

348
00:24:11,950 --> 00:24:13,827
- How are you, Congressman?
- Oh, I'm fine.

349
00:24:13,910 --> 00:24:16,122
It's good of you all to be here.
It's a big day.

350
00:24:16,205 --> 00:24:18,790
Listen, why don't the two
of you take my seats?

351
00:24:18,874 --> 00:24:20,459
Oh, thanks, but we're
already on the aisle.

352
00:24:20,542 --> 00:24:21,960
I insist.

353
00:24:22,044 --> 00:24:24,380
You'll be right in the president's
line of vision when he...

354
00:24:24,463 --> 00:24:26,257
says what you want to hear.

355
00:24:27,383 --> 00:24:29,176
Please. I can be anywhere.

356
00:24:30,261 --> 00:24:31,345
Okay.

357
00:24:32,638 --> 00:24:34,265
- Thank you, Mark.
- Sure.

358
00:24:38,394 --> 00:24:42,648
I, Francis J.
Underwood, do solemnly swear...

359
00:24:42,731 --> 00:24:45,859
I, Francis J. Underwood,
do solemnly swear...

360
00:24:45,942 --> 00:24:48,404
That I will faithfully execute...

361
00:24:48,487 --> 00:24:50,489
That I will faithfully execute...

362
00:24:50,572 --> 00:24:53,492
The office of President
of the United States.

363
00:24:53,575 --> 00:24:57,121
The office of Pres...
Yes, here we go again.

364
00:24:57,204 --> 00:24:59,581
Secretary of State.
That's what I wanted.

365
00:24:59,665 --> 00:25:02,918
That's all I wanted. That's
what I was promised.

366
00:25:03,502 --> 00:25:08,715
And now, here I am, President
of these United States.

367
00:25:10,342 --> 00:25:14,471
You made this bed, America.
You voted for me.

368
00:25:16,557 --> 00:25:18,642
Are you confused?

369
00:25:18,725 --> 00:25:20,644
Are you afraid?

370
00:25:20,727 --> 00:25:23,939
Because what you thought
you wanted is now here.

371
00:25:24,022 --> 00:25:29,236
And there you are, staring
back, slack-jawed, bewildered,

372
00:25:29,320 --> 00:25:32,323
wondering if this is what
you actually asked for.

373
00:25:32,406 --> 00:25:37,411
This democracy, your
democracy, elected me.

374
00:25:37,994 --> 00:25:39,580
And if you think it was
hard getting here,

375
00:25:39,663 --> 00:25:42,749
you're beginning to understand
what I'm willing to do to stay.

376
00:25:43,584 --> 00:25:46,212
I look across at this
crowd gathered today

377
00:25:46,295 --> 00:25:48,714
and I know that these
are not my supporters.

378
00:25:49,298 --> 00:25:51,717
I'm looking at people who are waiting,

379
00:25:51,800 --> 00:25:55,512
with a smile on their
face, for their turn.

380
00:25:55,596 --> 00:25:57,181
And the most vicious among them

381
00:25:57,264 --> 00:26:00,601
are the ones who are smiling
and clapping the hardest.

382
00:26:01,227 --> 00:26:04,521
Power is a lot like real estate.
Remember?

383
00:26:04,605 --> 00:26:07,399
- So help me God.
- So help me God.

384
00:26:26,752 --> 00:26:30,005
America, I hear you.

385
00:26:30,797 --> 00:26:33,342
I hear all your voices.

386
00:26:33,425 --> 00:26:37,053
I hear your struggles, your concerns,

387
00:26:37,138 --> 00:26:41,642
but most of all, I hear your hope.

388
00:26:41,725 --> 00:26:45,521
Opportunity and security
is what we all want.

389
00:26:45,604 --> 00:26:49,483
And in my first 100 days, I
will reach across party lines

390
00:26:49,566 --> 00:26:53,904
and devote my administration, your
government, to securing them.

391
00:26:53,987 --> 00:27:00,452
For too long we have ignored our seniors
and the healthcare they depend on.

392
00:27:02,496 --> 00:27:06,417
That is why I am committed
to fixing Medicare

393
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:09,753
before I devote one more
dime to the program.

394
00:27:09,836 --> 00:27:12,923
AmWorks was only the beginning.

395
00:27:13,006 --> 00:27:16,427
We have to learn to live
without entitlements,

396
00:27:16,510 --> 00:27:20,222
because we are not an entitled people.

397
00:27:20,306 --> 00:27:25,436
I believe in your sweat. I
believe in your hard work.

398
00:27:25,519 --> 00:27:29,231
And a job, that's what
I'll promise you today.

399
00:27:35,737 --> 00:27:37,239
<i>You
couldn't give him one line?</i>

400
00:27:37,323 --> 00:27:39,658
We give no quarter to
those that are disloyal.

401
00:27:39,741 --> 00:27:42,411
<i>The message sent today
wasn't just for him.</i>

402
00:27:42,494 --> 00:27:45,539
- Alex feeds off of retribution.
<i>- So do I.</i>

403
00:27:45,622 --> 00:27:48,083
<i>- Is he worth the fight?</i>
- There is no neutrality.

404
00:27:48,166 --> 00:27:51,127
I expect total loyalty
or you are an enemy.

405
00:27:52,213 --> 00:27:54,465
Understood, Mr. President.

406
00:27:59,345 --> 00:28:00,679
What are you thinking?

407
00:28:04,099 --> 00:28:06,560
<i>Let mortal tongues awake</i>

408
00:28:07,936 --> 00:28:10,397
<i>Let all that breathe partake</i>

409
00:28:11,482 --> 00:28:14,401
<i>Let rocks their silent break</i>

410
00:28:15,736 --> 00:28:18,447
I have a soft spot for
that patriotic stuff.

411
00:28:28,665 --> 00:28:31,209
Please spread your arms and legs.

412
00:28:31,293 --> 00:28:32,586
Excuse me?

413
00:28:32,669 --> 00:28:34,921
Otherwise, there is no interview.

414
00:28:40,886 --> 00:28:43,054
And if you would please
take off your jacket.

415
00:28:45,056 --> 00:28:46,725
- I'm a journalist.
- I know.

416
00:28:46,808 --> 00:28:48,310
I read you when I was living in London.

417
00:28:48,394 --> 00:28:51,688
- So I'm not a spy.
- I know you're not a spy.

418
00:28:51,772 --> 00:28:54,024
Does anyone even use that word anymore?

419
00:28:56,943 --> 00:28:58,987
So the interview won't
be happening here.

420
00:28:59,070 --> 00:28:59,867
Uh, we agreed...

421
00:28:59,892 --> 00:29:02,026
We need to move you
to another location.

422
00:29:02,533 --> 00:29:04,993
Oh, and I'm sorry, you
cannot use your own crew.

423
00:29:05,744 --> 00:29:08,747
- That's unacceptable.
- Then the interview will have to wait.

424
00:29:08,830 --> 00:29:10,707
We cannot trust your people.

425
00:29:10,791 --> 00:29:13,877
And you wouldn't want to endanger Mr.
Macallan, would you?

426
00:29:15,921 --> 00:29:17,964
Are you coming, Miss Baldwin?

427
00:29:24,763 --> 00:29:26,807
And your cell phone needs to stay here.

428
00:29:28,309 --> 00:29:29,976
Thank you.

429
00:29:38,819 --> 00:29:40,446
You have a lot of support out there,

430
00:29:40,529 --> 00:29:43,114
no matter what the president
did or didn't say.

431
00:29:43,198 --> 00:29:45,241
There are people eager to work with you.

432
00:29:46,285 --> 00:29:47,786
I should tell you...

433
00:29:48,704 --> 00:29:50,584
I'm encouraging the
Republicans to restart

434
00:29:50,609 --> 00:29:52,095
the Declaration of War Committee,

435
00:29:52,169 --> 00:29:54,088
to investigate the president.

436
00:29:55,126 --> 00:29:56,211
That's bold.

437
00:29:56,610 --> 00:29:58,164
I'm not trying to
impress you with that.

438
00:29:58,189 --> 00:29:59,196
<i>It's risky.</i>

439
00:30:01,174 --> 00:30:03,302
You don't want an enemy
in the White House.

440
00:30:04,135 --> 00:30:06,805
But you're in the White House now.

441
00:30:07,723 --> 00:30:09,140
And you're what to me?

442
00:30:11,518 --> 00:30:13,354
Why don't you let me help you?

443
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:18,484
Everybody doesn't have to
lose for you to win, right?

444
00:30:22,070 --> 00:30:23,489
<i>Is Sean Jeffries there?</i>

445
00:30:24,365 --> 00:30:27,117
- Who's calling?
<i>- Who's this?</i>

446
00:30:27,200 --> 00:30:29,160
I work for Tom Hammerschmidt.

447
00:30:29,244 --> 00:30:31,497
<i>I don't understand why I
was transferred to you.</i>

448
00:30:32,498 --> 00:30:35,626
The receptionist is a friend of mine.
I know him. She knows him.

449
00:30:35,709 --> 00:30:38,086
<i>Well, I've been trying
to reach him all day.</i>

450
00:30:38,169 --> 00:30:40,005
I can take a message.

451
00:30:40,088 --> 00:30:41,223
<i>No, I'll try him later.</i>

452
00:30:41,248 --> 00:30:43,880
Look, he doesn't work
here anymore, so...

453
00:30:45,218 --> 00:30:47,929
- I'm his girlfriend.
<i>- That's all right.</i>

454
00:30:49,305 --> 00:30:51,767
- Who is this?
- Don't worry about it.

455
00:30:59,024 --> 00:31:02,778
Hey, it's Seth Grayson.
I've got something for you.

456
00:31:02,861 --> 00:31:05,155
LeAnn Harvey, she's being pushed out.

457
00:31:06,114 --> 00:31:07,949
Yeah, she's out.

458
00:31:08,492 --> 00:31:13,705
Yeah, well, you're the only one that's
got me telling you at this point.

459
00:31:14,748 --> 00:31:17,834
Okay. I'm writing the release right now.

460
00:31:17,918 --> 00:31:19,127
All right, bye.

461
00:31:24,883 --> 00:31:27,052
Hey, it's Seth Grayson.

462
00:31:27,135 --> 00:31:28,554
Yeah, I got something for you.

463
00:31:33,809 --> 00:31:38,354
Kate Baldwin's people were ready to
go, but the FSB replaced the crew.

464
00:31:40,607 --> 00:31:42,651
You have something on your tooth.

465
00:31:50,116 --> 00:31:51,868
Do we know where they were going?

466
00:31:51,952 --> 00:31:54,245
No, but they're still being tracked.

467
00:31:54,329 --> 00:31:56,457
Kate Baldwin was never
supposed to talk to him.

468
00:31:56,540 --> 00:31:58,542
- This interview cannot happen.
- We still have time.

469
00:31:58,625 --> 00:32:00,419
No, we don't.

470
00:32:01,211 --> 00:32:02,337
We need to go in.

471
00:32:02,421 --> 00:32:04,423
And there might be some
unforeseen consequences.

472
00:32:08,218 --> 00:32:11,940
I don't think Aidan would reveal
anything that would endanger me...

473
00:32:11,965 --> 00:32:13,205
or anyone else.

474
00:32:14,725 --> 00:32:16,518
How far back do you and he go?

475
00:32:17,143 --> 00:32:18,061
Far.

476
00:32:20,355 --> 00:32:21,482
That's all.

477
00:32:23,066 --> 00:32:24,610
I'm sorry it has to end this way.

478
00:32:25,652 --> 00:32:27,446
I don't need your sympathy.

479
00:32:36,204 --> 00:32:37,581
You almost ready?

480
00:32:39,500 --> 00:32:44,880
God damn it. I can't find my "F."
Someone must have stolen it.

481
00:32:44,963 --> 00:32:47,549
I'm sure it will turn up somewhere.

482
00:32:47,633 --> 00:32:50,927
Tom, you look very handsome in a tuxedo.

483
00:32:51,845 --> 00:32:53,597
I act like a man that can wear a tuxedo

484
00:32:53,680 --> 00:32:56,600
and suddenly I'm a man
who can wear a tuxedo.

485
00:32:56,683 --> 00:32:58,977
This should do for now.

486
00:32:59,060 --> 00:33:02,105
- I just saw that Eric Rawlings arrived.
- Yes.

487
00:33:02,856 --> 00:33:05,901
Tom, we'll meet you downstairs
in just a few minutes.

488
00:33:05,984 --> 00:33:07,110
Sure.

489
00:33:09,696 --> 00:33:13,324
Stamper tells me that we'll have
a bead on Macallan's location

490
00:33:13,408 --> 00:33:14,993
in about an hour or so.

491
00:33:15,076 --> 00:33:19,289
I just spoke to Jane Davis. Her
contacts are ready and waiting.

492
00:33:20,290 --> 00:33:23,043
- We'll have distance, Francis.
- We'll owe her.

493
00:33:28,256 --> 00:33:31,134
You look very beautiful. Presidential.

494
00:33:32,135 --> 00:33:35,972
You look beautiful. Vice Presidential.

495
00:33:45,398 --> 00:33:48,527
Here we are. The
night of Zoe's death.

496
00:33:52,531 --> 00:33:54,115
Who does that look like to you?

497
00:33:54,783 --> 00:33:57,285
Just another white guy
waiting for the train.

498
00:33:58,829 --> 00:33:59,913
Look closer.

499
00:34:04,626 --> 00:34:08,797
I know who you think it might be, but...
I'm really not sure.

500
00:34:21,893 --> 00:34:23,436
Yeah, shut that door.

501
00:34:25,355 --> 00:34:31,069
Gerald Ford's son used to sneak up
these stairs to go to the roof,

502
00:34:31,152 --> 00:34:32,988
play Led Zeppelin to his friends.

503
00:34:33,071 --> 00:34:36,157
Teenagers are teenagers,
doesn't matter where.

504
00:34:36,241 --> 00:34:41,872
And this... is where Viktor
Petrov put out his cigar.

505
00:34:45,674 --> 00:34:47,853
That guy seems like a
real piece of work.

506
00:34:47,878 --> 00:34:49,379
Oh, believe me, he is.

507
00:34:51,632 --> 00:34:53,008
So are you.

508
00:34:53,925 --> 00:34:55,802
No, I don't know about that.

509
00:35:13,862 --> 00:35:16,698
- What are you doing?
- Oh, I'm sorry.

510
00:35:25,999 --> 00:35:27,626
And what are you sorry about?

511
00:35:30,796 --> 00:35:34,049
I thought you wanted
something that you don't.

512
00:35:36,509 --> 00:35:38,845
So what is it that you think I wanted?

513
00:35:38,929 --> 00:35:41,973
We can go back up.
I'm... I'm really s...

514
00:35:44,350 --> 00:35:48,313
You know, they say the most
vulnerable part of the human body...

515
00:35:50,065 --> 00:35:51,983
is the throat...

516
00:35:54,110 --> 00:35:58,364
and that you never really know
how you feel about somebody...

517
00:36:00,075 --> 00:36:04,495
until they have their
hands around your throat.

518
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:10,543
So tell me.

519
00:36:13,213 --> 00:36:15,131
How do you feel now?

520
00:36:29,187 --> 00:36:31,022
Congratulations, Mr. President.

521
00:36:48,707 --> 00:36:52,460
Before I forget, LeAnn Harvey
wanted me to say hello.

522
00:36:53,920 --> 00:36:55,588
How is she?

523
00:36:55,671 --> 00:36:56,965
Well, she wanted me to tell you

524
00:36:57,048 --> 00:37:00,093
that she's... she's all right.
She's gonna be all right.

525
00:37:02,178 --> 00:37:03,847
What does that mean?

526
00:37:05,724 --> 00:37:09,728
She's, um, taking a leave of absence.
Well, that's what they're calling it,

527
00:37:09,811 --> 00:37:13,982
but it... it seems that she's being
pushed out of the administration.

528
00:37:14,941 --> 00:37:15,791
Mmm-hmm.

529
00:37:15,816 --> 00:37:17,176
She's the one who told
me you were here.

530
00:37:17,235 --> 00:37:18,694
Suggested I talk to you.

531
00:37:20,947 --> 00:37:23,241
LeAnn hired you, isn't that right?

532
00:37:23,324 --> 00:37:24,617
Friend of the family?

533
00:37:26,828 --> 00:37:28,038
Uh...

534
00:37:29,122 --> 00:37:32,918
Yeah, um, are we starting
the interview now?

535
00:37:33,001 --> 00:37:36,421
No, no. We're just talking.
But we should be ready soon.

536
00:37:37,380 --> 00:37:38,757
I'm sorry.

537
00:37:38,840 --> 00:37:43,303
Can I, uh... Can I just have
a second before we start?

538
00:37:44,387 --> 00:37:46,597
- I think so. Sure. Um...
- Okay.

539
00:37:46,681 --> 00:37:50,435
Um... Yeah. I'm... I'm
gonna need some air.

540
00:37:58,484 --> 00:38:01,737
No, I just... I just
need a second, okay?

541
00:38:01,822 --> 00:38:04,449
Of course. Get some air.

542
00:38:04,532 --> 00:38:05,533
Okay.

543
00:38:50,996 --> 00:38:52,998
Hey, hey, hey.
I want your phone.

544
00:38:53,081 --> 00:38:56,126
I need your phone. Take this.

545
00:38:57,627 --> 00:38:59,170
One call.

546
00:39:01,006 --> 00:39:02,090
One.

547
00:39:15,561 --> 00:39:16,771
Hello?

548
00:39:16,855 --> 00:39:18,356
<i>LeAnn, it's me.</i>

549
00:39:21,067 --> 00:39:22,693
My God, Aidan.

550
00:39:22,777 --> 00:39:25,446
<i>I know you're in trouble.
Here's the plan.</i>

551
00:39:25,530 --> 00:39:27,448
<i>All right. Get a pen.
Write down this number.</i>

552
00:39:27,532 --> 00:39:29,951
<i>He... he's a lawyer. He can
get you out of the country.</i>

553
00:39:30,035 --> 00:39:32,370
<i>Once you're in Russia,
I can protect you.</i>

554
00:39:33,496 --> 00:39:34,705
I'm sorry.

555
00:39:35,373 --> 00:39:36,416
<i>Why?</i>

556
00:39:42,463 --> 00:39:43,882
Oh.

557
00:39:44,674 --> 00:39:48,970
<i>How long do I have
before they come and get me?</i>

558
00:39:50,555 --> 00:39:52,390
They're right outside.

559
00:39:52,473 --> 00:39:53,808
<i>And if I run?</i>

560
00:39:54,976 --> 00:39:58,063
Uh, people will get hurt.
You don't want to do that.

561
00:40:04,485 --> 00:40:06,196
I like Texas.

562
00:40:06,779 --> 00:40:08,156
<i>Maybe it's the sun or the dust,</i>

563
00:40:08,239 --> 00:40:10,992
<i>but people kind of squint
when they say hello.</i>

564
00:40:11,076 --> 00:40:12,535
<i>I always liked that.</i>

565
00:40:14,162 --> 00:40:15,788
<i>I never should've left.</i>

566
00:40:17,040 --> 00:40:18,959
You've always been very good to me.

567
00:40:19,042 --> 00:40:20,710
<i>I liked your mother.</i>

568
00:40:22,212 --> 00:40:24,172
<i>I liked her a lot.</i>

569
00:40:24,255 --> 00:40:26,424
I'm really sorry.

570
00:40:26,507 --> 00:40:27,675
Don't be.

571
00:40:28,509 --> 00:40:30,886
<i>There's a guard on the second
floor working with us.</i>

572
00:40:30,971 --> 00:40:33,598
<i>He'll escort you to a corner
where a cab will be waiting.</i>

573
00:40:33,681 --> 00:40:35,391
<i>Someone will open the door. Just...</i>

574
00:40:36,642 --> 00:40:37,978
<i>get in.</i>

575
00:40:38,061 --> 00:40:39,395
So you don't miss Texas?

576
00:40:40,688 --> 00:40:41,982
No, I do.

577
00:40:46,194 --> 00:40:48,279
<i>I'm trying
to find some peace with this</i>

578
00:40:48,363 --> 00:40:49,364
<i>and you're not helping.</i>

579
00:40:49,447 --> 00:40:52,075
- Just a couple more questions.
<i>- I'm moving, you know.</i>

580
00:40:52,951 --> 00:40:54,995
<i>- I got a new job...</i>
- Mr. Barnes?

581
00:40:55,078 --> 00:40:57,455
They ruled your daughter's
death an accident.

582
00:40:57,538 --> 00:40:58,914
I am starting to doubt that.

583
00:40:59,624 --> 00:41:01,780
<i>Could we just go over
one or two more...</i>

584
00:41:01,805 --> 00:41:02,900
You know what?

585
00:41:03,169 --> 00:41:05,171
I just can't keep doing this.

586
00:41:05,255 --> 00:41:07,382
Stop calling me, please.

587
00:41:15,931 --> 00:41:18,434
The interview
will not be happening.

588
00:41:18,518 --> 00:41:20,436
Someone will escort you
back to your hotel.

589
00:41:31,614 --> 00:41:32,865
Mr. President.

590
00:41:34,242 --> 00:41:35,493
I hope I'm not too late to the party.

591
00:41:35,576 --> 00:41:38,496
I'd say you were just in time
to make your grand entrance.

592
00:41:38,579 --> 00:41:41,207
Well, when all this started, I
don't think I imagined myself

593
00:41:41,291 --> 00:41:44,169
standing here with you tonight.

594
00:41:44,252 --> 00:41:46,254
- Congratulations, Mr. President. Again.
- Oh, thank you.

595
00:41:46,337 --> 00:41:48,381
Um, should I wait for you?

596
00:41:48,464 --> 00:41:50,633
No, no. I'll see you inside the party.

597
00:41:53,303 --> 00:41:55,763
So... how's Romero?

598
00:41:55,846 --> 00:41:58,016
Well, Romero wants to
get the Republicans

599
00:41:58,099 --> 00:42:00,726
to restart the Declaration
of War Committee.

600
00:42:00,810 --> 00:42:04,230
What a lovely inauguration gift.
Don't tell me you're surprised.

601
00:42:04,314 --> 00:42:05,815
I'm not.

602
00:42:05,898 --> 00:42:08,068
I'll see you inside. Get a drink.

603
00:42:13,073 --> 00:42:16,034
Well, don't you look stunning.

604
00:42:17,327 --> 00:42:19,412
It's been a long night, Frank.

605
00:42:19,495 --> 00:42:22,832
- I need to get home.
- Oh, come on. Do me the honor.

606
00:42:24,917 --> 00:42:26,001
Not tonight.

607
00:43:41,076 --> 00:43:42,703
No, absolutely.

608
00:43:46,499 --> 00:43:49,252
- Doug! Don't you look good.
- Thank you, Madam Vice President.

609
00:43:49,335 --> 00:43:51,921
- Have you seen Francis?
- Not for a little while, no, ma'am.

610
00:43:52,004 --> 00:43:54,840
Well, the party's breaking up. I
want him to say goodbye to people.

611
00:43:54,924 --> 00:43:56,842
- Yes, ma'am. I'll let him know.
- Um, Doug?

612
00:43:58,553 --> 00:44:00,638
I have a question for you.
Just a small thing.

613
00:44:01,222 --> 00:44:04,225
- What is it?
- Why weren't you at the beheading?

614
00:44:04,892 --> 00:44:07,145
- What?
- The night James Miller was beheaded.

615
00:44:08,103 --> 00:44:10,105
We were all there, the four of us.

616
00:44:10,190 --> 00:44:13,151
Francis, Tom, LeAnn and myself.

617
00:44:13,234 --> 00:44:14,485
Why not you?

618
00:44:16,154 --> 00:44:18,656
You see Francis, tell him
I'm looking for him.

619
00:44:18,739 --> 00:44:19,990
Yes, ma'am.

620
00:44:36,174 --> 00:44:40,511
- I thought you got lost.
- No. I just needed a break.

621
00:44:41,637 --> 00:44:45,099
- Shall we bid our guests farewell?
- No, the night is young.

622
00:44:47,101 --> 00:44:48,519
Look what I found.

623
00:44:59,697 --> 00:45:01,657
They're restarting the committee.

624
00:45:03,743 --> 00:45:05,620
I thought we killed that committee.

625
00:45:05,703 --> 00:45:08,373
Nothing ever
really dies in the House.

626
00:45:08,456 --> 00:45:13,127
It just plays in front of a
smaller and smaller audience.

627
00:45:16,381 --> 00:45:19,259
- This is serious.
- I know.

628
00:45:20,635 --> 00:45:23,137
But let's enjoy the rest of the party.

629
00:45:46,827 --> 00:45:47,953
Hey.

630
00:45:50,998 --> 00:45:52,958
What do you do here?

631
00:45:54,460 --> 00:45:55,753
I work for them.

632
00:46:00,007 --> 00:46:02,510
The president asked me to make
sure that you get home safely.

633
00:46:02,593 --> 00:46:03,710
I have a car for you.

634
00:46:03,968 --> 00:46:05,662
I thought I'd stay a
little while longer.

635
00:46:05,721 --> 00:46:08,308
The president has an early morning
tomorrow. Right this way.

636
00:46:30,996 --> 00:46:32,457
The tour guide?

637
00:46:36,502 --> 00:46:38,128
- What?
- Yes.

638
00:46:38,921 --> 00:46:42,717
WTF. What am I supposed to
do about the tour guide?

639
00:46:45,177 --> 00:46:47,763
The press briefing room, Tom, really?

640
00:46:51,392 --> 00:46:53,478
I didn't think anyone would care.

641
00:46:54,144 --> 00:46:57,732
Well, should I... keep
her on or let her go?

642
00:46:57,815 --> 00:47:01,318
I mean, it was rather
unprofessional on her part.

643
00:47:04,321 --> 00:47:06,115
She's good at her job.

644
00:47:09,660 --> 00:47:10,745
Tom...

645
00:47:12,079 --> 00:47:14,123
don't cheat on my wife.

646
00:47:37,938 --> 00:47:39,189
Mr. Barnes?

647
00:47:41,942 --> 00:47:45,946
Do you want to get a coffee
somewhere away from this?

648
00:47:47,448 --> 00:47:49,450
I keep thinking about what you said.

649
00:47:54,079 --> 00:47:56,081
I found this with her things.

650
00:47:58,167 --> 00:48:00,335
It was Father's Day...

651
00:48:00,420 --> 00:48:02,337
the last time I heard from her.

652
00:48:02,422 --> 00:48:03,964
We weren't close.

653
00:48:05,049 --> 00:48:06,801
I was surprised she called.

654
00:48:07,384 --> 00:48:10,095
I mean, I was happy, of course, but...

655
00:48:11,597 --> 00:48:13,223
Can I hold on to this?

656
00:48:18,479 --> 00:48:19,564
I didn't know...

657
00:48:21,774 --> 00:48:23,275
that she was in trouble.

658
00:48:26,070 --> 00:48:29,657
When the police asked if I thought
she was suicidal, I said, "Maybe."

659
00:48:34,537 --> 00:48:36,163
I didn't know her, really.

660
00:48:37,707 --> 00:48:39,750
Neither did I.

661
00:48:42,462 --> 00:48:43,879
I'll look into this.

662
00:48:46,423 --> 00:48:47,424
Okay.

663
00:48:48,718 --> 00:48:49,844
Thank you.

664
00:49:03,315 --> 00:49:06,276
<i>Politico.</i> Five minutes on <i>Morning Joe.</i>

665
00:49:06,360 --> 00:49:09,655
- Was a press release really necessary?
- You're taking a leave.

666
00:49:10,906 --> 00:49:12,575
The press release said that
you were taking a leave

667
00:49:12,658 --> 00:49:14,660
to consider your career options.

668
00:49:14,744 --> 00:49:17,121
This has to look real. You know that.

669
00:49:18,205 --> 00:49:19,665
What if they need to meet with me?

670
00:49:21,917 --> 00:49:23,252
Then they'll meet with you.

671
00:49:32,553 --> 00:49:34,889
I could have security escort you out.

672
00:49:43,438 --> 00:49:47,484
So since Usher is special advisor
now, where does that leave you?

673
00:49:50,946 --> 00:49:53,866
Like I said, they'll call
you if they need you.

674
00:49:59,204 --> 00:50:01,791
I can be a friend to you, you know.

675
00:50:02,958 --> 00:50:06,003
- It doesn't have to be like this.
- I don't have any friends.

676
00:50:22,227 --> 00:50:26,065
I landed him at the
Jordanian Embassy in Paris.

677
00:50:27,357 --> 00:50:28,943
When do we plan to get him out?

678
00:50:30,194 --> 00:50:32,863
Well, as I understand
it, you don't yet know

679
00:50:32,947 --> 00:50:35,783
what Mr. Macallan may have
told President Petrov.

680
00:50:35,866 --> 00:50:39,369
I want Macallan back
here sooner rather than later.

681
00:50:39,453 --> 00:50:42,414
It's better if these things
don't happen on American soil.

682
00:50:43,165 --> 00:50:47,336
- Yes, but I make those decisions.
- Of course, Mr. President.

683
00:50:48,671 --> 00:50:49,797
And rest assured,

684
00:50:49,880 --> 00:50:52,758
neither of your fingerprints
are anywhere near this.

685
00:51:00,975 --> 00:51:02,685
So what do we do about LeAnn?

686
00:51:05,145 --> 00:51:09,650
She's been involved in election
fraud, for Christ's sake.

687
00:51:10,901 --> 00:51:12,987
She's made a lot of mistakes.

688
00:51:14,238 --> 00:51:19,076
- Yes, but lots of people have.
- Yes, but some people run their course.

689
00:51:22,204 --> 00:51:24,456
It's hard to know who
to trust these days.

690
00:51:25,332 --> 00:51:26,500
Isn't it?

691
00:51:36,688 --> 00:51:41,688
